## Generation tips

A few practical notes for getting these to render well:

- **Negative prompt boilerplate** worth carrying across all generations: `cartoon, anime, cute, friendly, smooth gradients, plastic toy, calcium shells, coral, seashells, fish scales, dolphin, recognizable Earth fish anatomy, vertebrate skeleton, bones, cuddly, mascot`. The "no Earth fish" exclusions matter — these models default hard to bass and tuna shapes.
- **Aspect ratio.** 16:9 or 21:9 for the bigger pelagic/apex creatures (gives them room to feel large), 1:1 or 4:3 for benthic and swarm shots.
- **Model choice matters a lot here.** Flux and SDXL handle the "scientific specimen" register reasonably; Midjourney will lean more aesthetic-cinematic, which works for codex hero shots but fights you when you want naturalistic biology. If the model adds gills and dorsal fins anyway, lean harder on the chitin/silica/cephalopod vocabulary in the prompt and shorten the species-name framing — these models don't actually know what *Acidonecton vorax* is, and pretending they do can dilute the signal.
- **Codex hero shot variant.** For each creature, you can also try a clinical specimen-on-black-background variant: replace the SETTING block with `Specimen photography on pure black background, scientific museum lighting, three-quarter angle, centered composition, no environment.` Useful for the codex UI entries we sketched in the previous turn.

If a particular generation comes back with an awkward body plan and you want to lock it in for consistency across angles, do one good seed first then use that as image-to-image init for the remaining shots of the same species — much faster than re-rolling the prompt twenty times.